ed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p><strong>Omarion Hampton</strong> (10 carries, 43 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Ladd McConkey</strong> projects for <strong>5 targets and 41 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Oronde Gadsden II</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 29 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>DEN \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>DEN are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>J.K. Dobbins</strong> (12 carries, 61 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Courtland Sutton</strong> projects for <strong>6 targets and 51 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Evan Engram</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 21 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild a